Cogym discovers the best way to seed another model

This is the killer experiment.

Take a task dataset and two models:

TEACHER
produces seed

STUDENT
actually performs task

Try seeds:

A — nothing
B — normal advice
C — full verbose plan
D — concise plan
E — hypotheses
F — decision tree
G — dependency graph
H — uncertainty frontier
I — negative constraints
J — graph + priorities + unknowns
K — evolved Cogym format

Then look at the student's actual trajectory.

Not:

“Did you find this plan useful?”

Measure:

task success
number of actions
wrong turns
tool calls
retrieval calls
tokens
time
repeated work

That is an unusually clean science problem.

There is an especially cool hypothesis

Maybe the best seed isn't the answer.

Maybe it's:

{
  "search_space": [...],
  "highest_information_gain_action": "...",
  "dead_ends": [...],
  "unknowns": [...]
}

Because an LLM is already good at generating actions.

What it often lacks is orientation.

So you don't tell it:

do 1
then 2
then 3
then 4

You tell it:

HERE IS THE SHAPE OF THE PROBLEM.

Important region: A
Likely dead region: B
Critical uncertainty: C
Dependency: D → E
Best discriminator: F

And let the model reason from there.

That's much more interesting.

Then HydraDB becomes useful later

Every task teaches us something:

Task phenotype
      │
      ├── kernel representation used
      ├── model receiving kernel
      ├── actions taken
      ├── failure modes
      └── result

Over thousands of Cogym runs:

coding/debugging
→ uncertainty frontier works best

research
→ evidence graph works best

multi-hop information tasks
→ dependency DAG works best

simple tasks
→ NO SEED best

planning
→ constraints + branch points best

Now the miner learns:

task
 ↓
classify phenotype
 ↓
retrieve successful kernel patterns
 ↓
generate optimal seed

That would justify HydraDB.

Not because “graph databases are cool,” but because you're accumulating an empirical graph of:

task structure → context representation → downstream behavior.